@* A Villain the player made and the GM now holds, drawn back to the player as their nemesis.

   **The owner's ruling of 2026-10-01: the name and an effect, never the sheet.** "Just give them
   something they can see and go 'holy crap'" — so the name surfaces out of drifting smoke, and
   the stats stay the GM's. Specimen B of the pitch, the owner's choice of the two, with its two
   other answers: always the Villain's night palette, and either side may still end the
   membership.

   **Always night, whatever the reader's screen is.** `theme.css` gives `.nemesis` the villain-dark
   token set in every state, so this block is a window into somebody else's game on a Hero's page
   in daylight. Nothing here names a colour; the tokens do.

   **The name surfaces once and then stays legible**, and the smoke never crosses it — the veils
   sit below the text. Under reduced motion `app.css` switches the surfacing and both smoke loops
   off outright rather than letting the 0.01ms duration tokens turn an infinite drift into a
   flicker; the resting frame is the name clear and the smoke still. `ProofPages.TheNemesis`
   drives this in a browser beside three broken twins. *@

<div class="nemesis">
    <span class="nemesis-veil" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="nemesis-veil nemesis-veil-far" aria-hidden="true"></span>

    <span class="nemesis-kicker">Your nemesis</span>
    <b class="nemesis-name">@Name</b>
    <span class="nemesis-who">@Who</span>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>What the Villain is called — the label it was last sent under.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Name { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>The game that holds it, or null where the campaign has since been deleted.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Campaign { get; set; }

    private string Who => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Campaign)
        ? "Yours once. The GM holds it now."
        : $"Yours once. The GM of {Campaign} holds it now.";
}
